Enrolling DC1 as an Agent

Windows agent install, pointed at the manager's internal IP:

powershell
Invoke-WebRequest -Uri https://packages.wazuh.com/4.x/windows/wazuh-agent-4.9.0-1.msi -OutFile $env:tmp\wazuh-agent.msi
msiexec.exe /i $env:tmp\wazuh-agent.msi /q WAZUH_MANAGER='172.16.0.10' WAZUH_AGENT_NAME='DC1'
NET START WazuhSvc
Issue hit and resolved: DNS resolution failure on the DC

Invoke-WebRequest failed with The remote name could not be resolved: 'packages.wazuh.com'. This is expected behavior for a domain controller running its own DNS Server role — DC1's DNS client points at itself, and a fresh DNS Server role has no forwarder configured for external names by default, so it can resolve the domain zone but nothing on the internet.

Fix: added public forwarders (8.8.8.8, 1.1.1.1) under DNS Manager → server properties → Forwarders tab. This is also a better long-term fix than routing around DNS, since it's a constraint that would resurface for any other tool pulled onto DC1 later.

